Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 p20:{plain:{version:"How therapist and client work together, agreeing on goals and tasks and trusting each other, predicts outcome across all therapies. Empathy, warmth and genuineness matter. For Thomas, love means willing another's good; the therapeutic relationship is a professional, one-sided form of this, with boundaries that protect it.",question:"Why does the relationship matter so much in therapy?",one:"The alliance reliably predicts outcome; it is benevolence ordered to the client's good."},
  hook:"\"To love is to wish good to someone\" (I-II q.26 a.4).",
  analogy:{title:"Climbing with a guide",text:"A mountain guide and a climber must agree on the summit (goal), the route (tasks), and trust each other on the rope (bond). If any of the three fails, the climb stalls, however good the gear."},
  sort:{title:"Goal, task or bond?",cats:["Goal","Task","Bond"],items:[["Fewer panic attacks",0],["Weekly exposure homework",1],["Client trusts therapist",2],["Return to work",0],["Thought records",1],["Feeling understood",2]]}},
 p4l2:{plain:{version:"Psychotherapy works for most common problems, roughly as well as many medical treatments. The relationship and a credible plan matter in all therapies, while some problems also need specific techniques. Some therapists get better results, and some clients get worse, so measuring progress each session helps.",question:"Does therapy actually work, and does the type matter?",one:"Yes; relationship matters in all, and specific methods matter for some problems; measure progress."},
  hook:"\"Everybody has won, and all must have prizes\" (the dodo, quoted by Rosenzweig).",
  analogy:{title:"The cook and the recipe",text:"A good meal needs both a skilled cook and a sound recipe. Common factors are the cook; specific techniques are the recipe. For some dishes (OCD, PTSD), the recipe really matters."},
  sort:{title:"Common factor or specific ingredient?",cats:["Common factor","Specific ingredient"],items:[["Therapeutic alliance",0],["Exposure and response prevention",1],["Credible rationale",0],["Sleep restriction in CBT-I",1],["Empathy",0],["Behavioural parent training",1]]}},
 p4l3:{plain:{version:"Ethical practice means informed consent, confidentiality with clear legal limits (child abuse, sexual offences against children, serious danger, court orders), POPIA-compliant records, and boundaries that never exploit clients. The seal of confession is different: it has no exceptions. Professional ethics is a form of justice and fidelity.",question:"When can a therapist break confidentiality?",one:"With consent, for child abuse and certain sexual offences, serious danger, or court order; explain this at the start."},
  hook:"\"He that is faithful concealeth the thing committed to him\" (Prov 11:13).",
  analogy:{title:"The fence on a cliff",text:"Boundaries are like a fence along a cliff path: they don't restrict the walk, they make it safe. Small gaps (crossings) may be fine; removing the fence (violations) invites disaster."},
  sort:{title:"Boundary crossing or violation?",cats:["Crossing","Violation"],items:[["Accepting a small handmade card",0],["Business deal with a client",1],["Attending a client's graduation (discussed)",0],["Romantic relationship with a client",1],["Brief greeting at church",0],["Borrowing money from a client",1]]}},
 p4l4:{plain:{version:"For religious clients who want it, therapy that includes their faith works as well for symptoms and better for spiritual wellbeing. Faith can help (seeking God's love, community) or hurt (believing God is punishing). Therapists should ask about spirituality, integrate it only with consent and competence, and never proselytise.",question:"Should my therapist bring my faith into therapy?",one:"If you want it and they are competent: yes, it helps fit and spiritual wellbeing."},
  hook:"\"Whatsoever things are true… think on these things\" (Phil 4:8).",
  analogy:{title:"Speaking the client's language",text:"Therapy in a client's mother tongue is more fitting than therapy in a second language. For a deeply religious client, faith is a kind of mother tongue for meaning."},
  sort:{title:"Positive or negative religious coping?",cats:["Positive","Negative"],items:[["Seeking God's help and love",0],["'God is punishing me'",1],["Support from congregation",0],["'God will fix it; I'll do nothing'",1],["'God and I will face this together'",0],["Conflict with church leaders",1]]}},
 p4l5:{plain:{version:"Couple therapy works well: couples improve a lot while waiting-list couples don't. Many conflicts are cycles where one partner pursues and the other withdraws, both out of fear. Criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling harm marriages; gentleness, appreciation and responsibility heal. Where there is violence, safety comes first.",question:"Can couple therapy save a marriage?",one:"Often yes; but where there is violence or coercive control, safety comes first."},
  hook:"\"Charity is patient, is kind\" (1 Cor 13:4).",
  analogy:{title:"The dance",text:"A distressed couple is like two dancers stuck in a pattern: one steps forward, the other steps back, faster and faster. Therapy helps them see the dance and learn new steps together."},
  sort:{title:"Gottman's horsemen: which one?",cats:["Criticism","Contempt","Defensiveness","Stonewalling"],items:[["'You always forget everything'",0],["Rolling eyes and sarcasm",1],["'It's not my fault, you started it'",2],["Walking out silently",3],["Mocking the partner's family",1],["Refusing to respond",3]]}}
});
